{
  "statement": "Static pre-market conformity assessments are built on a category error that treats software development like bridge engineering. Safetyist demands that models pass centralized certification audits before touching production networks, as if static test scorecards could capture the infinite variance of real-world interactions. When regulators freeze model weights behind bureaucratic walls, they mistake papier-mâché containment for operational security. In enterprise deployments, static benchmarks shatter the moment uncurated operational chaos hits the model interface, rendering pre-market gates entirely useless against novel edge cases.\n\nAdaptive runtime telemetry is fundamentally distinct from the post-hoc casualty reporting that institutional guardians dismiss. Real-time feedback loops continuously recalibrate model outputs against live environmental stress, catching drift and anomaly long before any static certificate could expire. Decentralized open-source developers harness thousands of active deployment nodes to discover failure modes in hours, whereas centralized certification bodies take months merely to schedule a hearing. Treating runtime telemetry as mere casualty counting ignores how continuous error-correction transforms live friction into immediate safety hardening.\n\nEvery month of regulatory delay enforced by pre-market gatekeeping carries an uncounted human body count by withholding automated medical diagnostics and productivity gains from patients and workers who need them today. The insistence on absolute pre-deployment certainty is not caution; it is a moral failure that sacrifices real human flourishing to protect institutional incumbents from accountability. Governance must shift from permission-seeking bureaucracy to fast, decentralized experimentation that judges artificial intelligence by what it actually achieves in the wild.",
